|||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||
|
| LAB #7 - Problem 3: In-Place Array Sorting
| Group 9
| Cabrera, Niles
| Nellas, Aldrin
| Tibayan, Rafael
|
| Sorts the N values stored after ARRAY into ascending order, in place,
| using bubble sort. ARRAY[i] below means the i-th value, the word at
| ARRAY + 4 + 4i (the length cell itself is at ARRAY).
|
| Method (bubble sort):
|       Each pass walks the unsorted part, comparing neighbours and swapping
|       any pair that is out of order. After a pass, the largest remaining
|       value has moved to the end of the unsorted part, so the next pass
|       checks one value fewer (R0 - 1). Sorting stops early when a pass
|       makes no swaps (R1 = 0), because the array is then already sorted.
|
| Registers:
|       R0 = size of the unsorted part (starts at N)
|       R1 = N <= 1 flag at the start, then the swapped flag for each pass
|       R2 = address of ARRAY[i], the current element
|       R3 = comparisons left in this pass (starts at R0 - 1)
|       R4 = ARRAY[i]
|       R5 = ARRAY[i+1]
|       R6 = 1 if ARRAY[i] <= ARRAY[i+1] (pair already in order)
|       R7 = 1 if the unsorted part has 1 or fewer values left
|
| Edge cases:
|       N <= 1 is already sorted, so the program halts right away. The guard
|       also protects the inner loop, which tests its counter only after the
|       body runs: with N = 0 or 1, R3 = N - 1 would start at -1 or 0, go
|       negative, and the loop would compare and swap past the end of the
|       block.
|       CMPLE is a signed comparison, so negative numbers sort correctly.
|       Equal neighbours are not swapped (<=), so duplicates cause no extra
|       work and equal values keep their order.
|       Worst case (reverse order) is about N^2/2 comparisons; N = 100 in
|       reverse order takes about 50,000 instructions in BSim.
|       Labels are used as 16-bit signed constants in LD and CMOVE, so the
|       data must stay below address 0x8000.
|
| In BSim (with the data below): the code uses 0x000-0x053, the length cell
| is at 0x19C and the values are at 0x1A0-0x1BB.
|
|||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||

.include beta.uasm          | include the standard predefined macros

| Initialization
LD(R31, ARRAY, R0)      | R0 = N (array length; this cell is only read)
CMPLEC(R0, 1, R1)       | Check if N <= 1
BT(R1, DONE)            | If N <= 1, array is already sorted

OUTER_LOOP:
    CMOVE(0, R1)        | R1 = swapped flag (0 = false)
    CMOVE(ARRAY+4, R2)  | R2 = address of the first array element
    SUBC(R0, 1, R3)     | R3 = inner loop counter (R0 - 1 comparisons)

INNER_LOOP:
    LD(R2, 0, R4)       | R4 = current element (ARRAY[i])
    LD(R2, 4, R5)       | R5 = next element (ARRAY[i+1])

    CMPLE(R4, R5, R6)   | R6 = 1 if ARRAY[i] <= ARRAY[i+1] (signed)
    BT(R6, SKIP_SWAP)   | If already in ascending order, skip the swap

    | Swap the elements (the only stores; both stay inside the block)
    ST(R5, 0, R2)       | ARRAY[i] = R5
    ST(R4, 4, R2)       | ARRAY[i+1] = R4
    CMOVE(1, R1)        | Set swapped flag to 1 (true)

SKIP_SWAP:
    ADDC(R2, 4, R2)     | Advance R2 to the next element address
    SUBC(R3, 1, R3)     | Decrement the inner loop counter
    BNE(R3, INNER_LOOP) | If counter != 0, repeat INNER_LOOP

    SUBC(R0, 1, R0)     | Optimization: last element is now sorted, R0--
    CMPLEC(R0, 1, R7)   | Check if remaining unsorted N <= 1
    BT(R7, DONE)        | If so, sorting is complete
    BNE(R1, OUTER_LOOP) | If a swap occurred (R1 == 1), repeat OUTER_LOOP

DONE:
    HALT()              | stop the simulator

|||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||

| Data starts at a fixed address so it is easy to find in the BSim window.
. = 0x19C

| ARRAY holds N followed by the N values; the program sorts them in place.
ARRAY:
    LONG(7)             | Array length N (never written)
    LONG(5)
    LONG(7)
    LONG(8)
    LONG(6)
    LONG(4)
    LONG(9)
    LONG(3)
